From: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sunxi: h700: leave VDD-DRAM to the SPL

The SPL powers the DRAM it detected with boot0's voltage (DDR3 1.5 V,
LPDDR3 1.2 V, LPDDR4 1.1 V), but U-Boot's device tree fixed vdd-dram
(AXP717 DCDC3) at 1.1 V with min = max: the regulator uclass then set
1.1 V right after the SPL, on every board, and Linux (same tree) did it
again. The RG CubeXX has LPDDR3 (JEDEC VDD2/VDDQ 1.14-1.30 V): under the
heaviest GPU and memory load it flipped single bits in kernel pointers
and page tables (oopses in fresh panfrost job objects, user page-table
entries with stray high bits, whole-system hangs), about one God of War
PSP launch in 15-50; at 1.14 V 100 launches ran clean.

A range covering every type keeps the SPL's value (no autoset without
min = max). The stock firmware leaves this rail to boot0 too.

Signed-off-by: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
---
--- a/dts/upstream/src/arm64/allwinner/sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-2024.dts
+++ b/dts/upstream/src/arm64/allwinner/sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-2024.dts
@@ -271,8 +271,13 @@
 
 			reg_dcdc3: dcdc3 {
 				regulator-always-on;
+				/*
+				 * The SPL sets VDD-DRAM for the DRAM type it
+				 * detected: a range, so U-Boot (and Linux) keep
+				 * it instead of setting one voltage on every board.
+				 */
 				regulator-min-microvolt = <1100000>;
-				regulator-max-microvolt = <1100000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <1500000>;
 				regulator-name = "vdd-dram";
 			};
 
